WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.570
I love Mark Wahlberg, but
at the rate he's swinging,

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:09.287
it would take him three f---ing days

00:00:09.287 --> 00:00:10.878
to draw that steel out.

00:00:10.878 --> 00:00:12.720
Hi, my name is Neil Kamimura.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.360
I'm a bladesmith-blacksmith from Hawaii.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.210
My business name is T Kamimura Blacksmith.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.652
It was established in 1932,
Hilo, from my great-grandfather.

00:00:21.652 --> 00:00:23.118
Today, we're going to be looking at some

00:00:23.118 --> 00:00:25.352
blacksmithing scenes
and some forging scenes

00:00:25.352 --> 00:00:29.776
of some movies, and we're
going to see how real they are.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.170
Robert Downey Jr. has
the best hammer swing.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.820
It looked like he had practiced.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:41.040
He was standing close to the anvil,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.180
and he's basically
cold-forging mild steel.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.300
So, cold forging is not using heat

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.000
to make it pliable like clay;

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.090
he's actually just hitting steel cold.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.981
I don't quite understand,

00:00:56.981 --> 00:01:00.656
for one, why he has the
forge lit in the back,

00:01:00.656 --> 00:01:02.160
since he's cold-forging it.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:08.250
I don't know what he was doing,

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.250
'cause the steel had no color to it.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:13.770
He was probably forging it at low heat,

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.450
just getting it to work.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:17.580
But he's cooling it off
because it looks cool.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:19.256
They love the steam.

00:01:24.242 --> 00:01:26.662
It looks like he's holding a cup of tea

00:01:26.662 --> 00:01:29.904
because the crucible isn't hot.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.650
I personally don't think it
would create a perfect ring.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:42.090
It would have to be a wax mold

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:45.900
that you cast from both sides with sand.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.510
It wouldn't just be
pouring some hot tea on it

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.826
and then it makes a perfect, polished,

00:01:51.826 --> 00:01:53.640
electronic-ready ring.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.781
I would give this a 7,

00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:57.256
only because I like "Iron Man."

00:02:01.484 --> 00:02:03.457
Rambo: You know what you are;

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:05.960
what you're made of.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.168
Oh, boy. This is the one that

00:02:08.168 --> 00:02:10.776
I absolutely hate the most.

00:02:10.776 --> 00:02:13.230
And it's not because I don't
like "Rambo." I love "Rambo."

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.910
But the problem is that,
for one, that's a real shop.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.030
That's a shop that
somebody's worked out of,

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.460
so there was somebody in that vicinity

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.110
that knows what they're doing.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.093
Two, Sylvester Stallone

00:02:27.093 --> 00:02:31.293
sells a "Rambo" knife every release,

00:02:31.293 --> 00:02:34.170
and he has knife-maker friends.

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:35.670
I'm friends with Jason Momoa;

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.580
I would never let him do
a forging scene like this

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.200
in a million years.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.290
And he's using a ball-peen hammer,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.090
like, out of a mechanic's chest.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:49.020
A ball-peen hammer is a
pound, or maybe 12 ounces.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.730
My hammer's 4 pounds.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:52.680
And I'm pretty sure he
likes to lift weights,

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:56.013
so I don't know why he
wouldn't use a real hammer.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.440
Rambo: What you're made of.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.910
See, right there, he just
keeps hitting it on the end.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:06.660
All he's doing is putting
square hardy holes,

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.090
markings in the bottom of that thing.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:11.340
So, here's your anvil, right?

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.720
You have the horn.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.962
So sexy, right? You got this horn,

00:03:14.962 --> 00:03:18.000
you got this nice curve
on the back, right?

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.717
And then you have the
center mass in the middle.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:25.050
Then there is a 1-inch square hardy hole,

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:28.560
and that is meant for
putting tools in there.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.660
You have to remember
that the steel is hot,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.310
and you're just banging on a square hole,

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:33.469
you'll flip it over and you just have

00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:35.693
square-hole marks all over it.

00:03:35.693 --> 00:03:36.750
I don't know why they don't tell him,

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.360
"Hey, buddy, hit on the
center of the anvil.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.185
Why do you keep hitting it over the hole?

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:45.254
Rambo: When you're pushed.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.160
When you take steel at that temperature

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.680
and you quench it in
water, it microfractures.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:55.200
5,160 should be quenched in oil.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.700
But it doesn't need to be quenched,

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.820
because he's obviously not done forging it

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.100
and didn't make anything but a pancake

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.020
on the end of a leaf spring.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.630
Two, it's some magical water,

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.210
because he quenches it in
water and he lifts it up

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.318
and it's still hot,

00:04:11.318 --> 00:04:14.409
which, that is also not realistic.

00:04:14.409 --> 00:04:17.190
You put it in water, it would
lose its heat immediately.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.615
Maybe he just sucks at forging so bad

00:04:19.615 --> 00:04:22.790
that it was so thick that
he didn't do anything to it,

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:24.030
so it still held its heat.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.640
I hold this to a higher accountability,

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.790
as every "Rambo" movie,

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:31.923
he's forging.

00:04:31.923 --> 00:04:33.030
How many "Rambo"s is there?

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.940
Like, five f---ing "Rambo"s?

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:39.930
How has no one been like, "Hey,
buddy, you absolutely suck.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:41.880
You should not do those scenes anymore"?

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.060
Or, "Go take one lesson to do it."

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:46.710
I give it a 1. F--- "Rambo."

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.176
Like, I love the movie,

00:04:48.176 --> 00:04:50.626
I just don't like the forging scenes.

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.837
So, the fact that he
could take the handle off

00:04:59.837 --> 00:05:01.560
with two taps of a hammer,

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.990
it definitely wouldn't
have survived battle,

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.300
as you are hitting edge to edge.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.700
But at least they took the
handle off on this one.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.040
Some of them are pinned, and
a lot of them are through,

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:13.890
and then the pommel is what holds it on.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:16.140
But you definitely can't just take it

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:17.973
and be like

00:05:17.973 --> 00:05:19.564
and then it comes off.

00:05:19.564 --> 00:05:20.404
You know what I mean?

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:29.010
So, when you're melting steel

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:32.820
in an open-faced crucible
like that they were doing,

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.700
one, there wouldn't be
just, like, orangey flames.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:39.780
It would be the most
insane, looking at the sun.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:43.755
That's one. Two, the
actual crucible itself

00:05:43.755 --> 00:05:44.971
would need to be hot.

00:05:44.971 --> 00:05:48.450
That is just black, so it didn't heat up,

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:52.710
but yet somehow, the sword is
melting on a black surface,

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:56.669
which is the most ridiculous thing ever.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:04.594
Swords that were poured

00:06:04.594 --> 00:06:07.886
and casted like that were bronze.

00:06:07.886 --> 00:06:09.060
They weren't steel.

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:11.010
It takes about 3,000 degrees

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.170
to melt steel, that kind of steel.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.600
Bronze is a lower melting temperature,

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.610
and so that was a lot
easier to melt and cast,

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:21.000
but when they do cast it,
it wasn't in an open face.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.510
On both sides was it casted,
so that you pour it in the top

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:26.550
and it fills up and makes a perfect mold.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:29.820
The sword would look like
a cookie on a baking sheet,

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:32.503
so that's just bulls---.

00:06:32.503 --> 00:06:34.386
I don't quite understand

00:06:34.386 --> 00:06:37.578
how they got one sword and melted it,

00:06:37.578 --> 00:06:41.777
and you've somehow made
enough to make two swords now.

00:06:41.777 --> 00:06:44.518
You actually lose mass

00:06:44.518 --> 00:06:47.051
as you melt steel like that.

00:06:47.051 --> 00:06:51.249
I would definitely give that a 4.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.725
The thing that I did like about this scene

00:07:01.725 --> 00:07:06.600
is that they had it actually
in a cast, in a mold,

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.690
and he was breaking the mold out of it.

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.160
There's many different techniques.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.768
You can cast an ax because it's

00:07:14.768 --> 00:07:18.159
generally not made out
of very great steel,

00:07:18.159 --> 00:07:20.310
'cause it needs to take a lot of abuse,

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:23.850
versus, like, a chef knife
has to be the highest quality

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:27.810
of carbon steel because it
needs to be razor sharp.

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:31.830
Axes are brute force
that needs to be tough

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.471
versus razor sharp.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.300
I've never seen a steel butt forge weld

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:46.440
like that in one hit,
and it wasn't hot enough.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:51.302
And if you ever put wood
next to something that hot,

00:07:51.302 --> 00:07:52.710
it would disintegrate.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:55.500
You don't hold it to such
a high accountability,

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.270
because it's not real.

00:07:57.270 --> 00:08:00.952
A tree dude is giving
his arm for a handle.

00:08:00.952 --> 00:08:04.102
I mean, you can't take it too seriously.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:08.250
I don't understand why he
didn't just cast it in one,

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:10.530
but there's no way you could
forge-weld something like that.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:11.580
6.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:12.830
I don't know.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:24.750
For all his big muscles,
and I love Mark Wahlberg,

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:26.250
but at the rate he's swinging,

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.950
it would take him three f---ing days

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:32.412
to draw that steel out without any breaks.

00:08:32.412 --> 00:08:35.130
Like, literally, it
would take him three days

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:36.900
at that hammer swing.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:39.321
It's the weakest swing I've --

00:08:39.321 --> 00:08:40.980
I mean, it's pretty bad.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:44.213
He loves the hole too. You see
him hitting it on the hole?

00:08:45.750 --> 00:08:47.661
They all love that hole.

00:08:51.182 --> 00:08:55.620
Nobody wears a glove on
their hammer hand, ever,

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:59.370
because it's dangerous to
hold a hammer in that hand

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:02.250
with a glove, because it will
just fly out of your hand.

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:05.940
But if you are going to wear
gloves in a blacksmith shop,

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.440
they need to be a glove

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.590
that you can shake like
this and they fly off,

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.980
because once that leather gets so hot,

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.890
it will cook your hand
internally within seconds.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:20.640
You see people freak out, and
they can't get the glove off.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:24.063
It will cook your hand like an oven.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.623
Evan: Are there things
you just know how to do?

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.060
Right there. You see, generally,

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:32.250
you don't quench "finished" forged blades

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:35.280
that look like a road
going up to a mountain.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.250
That thing curvy as, crooked as can be.

00:09:38.250 --> 00:09:42.120
Once it quenches like that, it's done.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:44.790
You'll have to slowly take the work --

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:48.660
nobody ever would quench a crooked blade.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.690
Two, he's only heating the tip.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:54.931
So, basically,

00:09:54.931 --> 00:09:57.514
he's heating this much right here,

00:09:57.514 --> 00:10:00.030
so only this part of
the blade would be hot.

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:02.550
This would all be soft,
and it would catch a bend,

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:03.840
it would catch a warp.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:07.020
Traditionally, in Japanese weapons,

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.930
the blade is straight as forged.

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.140
There's a clay on the
back to prevent the spine

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.960
from getting hard, so it
protects it from the heat,

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:19.830
and then only the edge is exposed.

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:21.330
The edge is getting hard,

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:24.360
and that's what creates
the natural curvature

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:25.770
of a samurai sword.

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:29.010
So you do not go in f---ing
all bent and crooked, for one,

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.470
and two, you don't forge the bend in.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.090
It's actually quenched,

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:35.915
and through the heat-treating
process does the blade bend.

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.690
This is the best part of all time,

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.619
where he cuts a steel pipe.

00:10:41.619 --> 00:10:43.535
[laughs]

00:10:43.535 --> 00:10:46.470
Swords do not cut pipe.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.503
People think that samurai swords

00:10:48.503 --> 00:10:51.278
are the baddest things on the planet,

00:10:51.278 --> 00:10:54.750
and they are, for their time.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:57.223
But that doesn't necessarily
mean that it was the toughest

00:10:57.223 --> 00:11:00.180
and that it can cut through
cement and cut through --

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:02.520
no, they were actually,
some of them had a lot

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:06.990
of mild steel in them. A lot
of them caught bends, broke.

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:10.470
And this movie is about him
doing it traditional, right?

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:13.200
That's not how traditional
Japanese bladesmiths

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:14.520
test a finished product.

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:18.030
And they are a culture
so based out of respect,

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:20.850
you're not supposed to even
touch the steel with your hand.

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:24.692
They would not take it and
hit a f---ing pipe with it.

00:11:24.692 --> 00:11:26.970
It would be the ultimate
disrespect for them.

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:30.235
I'll give him a 2.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:38.400
If you look at the first initial scene,

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:40.590
there's one, two, three, four, five,

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:42.870
six pieces of broken sword,

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:44.520
and then when they go
to put it back together,

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:45.930
it's only two pieces.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:48.360
So, flux is, it's like borax.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:51.660
So basically, as it heats,
it's almost like melted glass

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:55.890
that cleans it so that it
allows it to stick together.

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:58.890
As you're forging
something on a flat surface

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:00.060
and you're hitting it with a hammer,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:02.040
it would never maintain that fuller.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:06.660
And the fuller is that curved spot

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:08.580
down the middle of the
blade. It's on both sides,

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:12.000
so as you hit it, it
would just flatten it out.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.310
The correct way to fix
a broken blade like this

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:19.500
would be to take the
handle and the guard off,

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:23.220
cut it up into smaller
pieces, add more material,

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:26.190
and forge-weld it again
and forge a new blade

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:38.670
You can just tell by their swing

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:41.010
that they've never touched
a hammer in their life.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.990
And it's not nearly hot enough

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:46.140
to just butt-weld a forged blade together.

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:50.370
To forge-weld, it's at
2,300. It's a brighter color.

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:55.370
It's not orangey, it's white.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:58.138
6, because they were actually

00:12:58.138 --> 00:13:01.378
forcing air into a coal forge.

00:13:01.378 --> 00:13:04.491
They were putting flux on
there to try and forge-weld it.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:11.520
So another, once again,
another open-face mold.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.140
And then he forges it.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.082
I have no idea what he's doing

00:13:16.082 --> 00:13:19.770
or what kind of oil they put
on there to make it do that.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:23.910
They're putting either
grease or wax or oil,

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:25.260
and they're putting it on the hammer

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.570
so it creates this whole fire,

00:13:27.570 --> 00:13:29.820
but they don't care about whether

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:31.920
or not the steel was actually hot enough

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.255
or where he's hitting it on the anvil.

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:38.280
Look at this! Just the tip is hot, look.

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:40.773
And then we're going to
quench this sword into snow.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:43.230
Oh, Lord.

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:47.520
So, what happens is, when
you take a sword like this

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:49.323
and you quench it into oil,

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.607
the reason why they move it up and down

00:13:52.607 --> 00:13:54.010
as they're quenching it

00:13:54.010 --> 00:13:56.820
is because what it does is it's so hot,

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:00.330
it creates a vapor jacket
that comes around it,

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.820
and so it's boiling all
around it, so it's vapor,

00:14:02.820 --> 00:14:04.980
so you're not actually rapid-cooling it

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:09.060
unless you're introducing
it into new oil, right?

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:11.160
So quenching it into snow,

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:12.990
it would just instantly melt around it

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:14.674
and then not touch it at all.

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:23.730
I still sharpen knives on a stone, too.

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:26.010
Maybe not a stone like that,

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.042
but, I mean, they
probably just picked it up

00:14:28.042 --> 00:14:30.030
and was like, [laughs]
you know, I don't know.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:34.020
I would rate that a 5 just
because it was from the '80s

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:36.805
and they didn't know any better.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.080
As she's hitting the anvil,

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:46.058
she's like, tap-tap, then hits the anvil.

00:14:46.058 --> 00:14:47.970
Like, you're not even
swinging it that hard.

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:50.310
You don't need to pump your brakes.

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:52.200
It's the most ridiculous thing ever.

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.960
If you ever see a video of me forging,

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:58.200
I'm swinging a 3- to 5-pound hammer,

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:01.298
and, yes, sometimes when I move the blade,

00:15:01.298 --> 00:15:04.373
I have to keep my hand
continuing to swing.

00:15:08.490 --> 00:15:12.570
I don't understand how
she's engraving with a nail.

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.970
It's, like, a log-cabin nail.

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:16.920
So, this right here is my forging stamp.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:20.850
It's done hot, and it's pressed in.

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:22.590
I just hit it with a hammer.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:24.968
Yeah, I give it a 4.

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:38.100
It wasn't hot enough at all to forge-weld,

00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.650
and that's basically what
they're trying to replicate.

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:43.620
As you're forging it
and you keep folding it,

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:45.450
it takes out the impurities.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:48.110
They're trying to hit it,

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:50.783
and they can't even keep it on the anvil.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.270
But how they turn that little puck

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:58.860
into a half-inch-thick plate
with perfect square edges

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:02.010
when they swing a hammer
like that is beyond me.

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:05.970
And so why make a plate that's
bigger than the anvil, right?

00:16:05.970 --> 00:16:07.200
You can't forge-weld it.

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:09.060
Look at it. It's just rocking on its own.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:12.030
You only want to open
it so much to fold it,

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:15.360
because how would you
create enough even pressure

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:17.340
to forge-weld that together?

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:20.040
You would hit it here, and
then it would forge-weld here

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:21.600
and then break loose here.

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:22.680
You would hit it here.

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:25.680
You want constant pressure of reduction.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:26.970
I would rate this a 3.

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:28.320
I don't have a favorite of any of them.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:31.353
I don't think movies in itself

00:16:31.353 --> 00:16:34.111
have ever captured it.

00:16:34.111 --> 00:16:36.960
It's just annoying because it affects me.

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:39.660
They're like, "All you
do is pour the knife.

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:40.650
It's not that hard."

00:16:40.650 --> 00:16:42.960
And I'm like, "B---, I swing
a hammer for a living."

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.300
I sacrifice my body to swing a hammer.

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:47.970
They think I just pour
it in a mold, you know?

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:51.120
So, hopefully now, this
gets enough exposure

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.570
where they might actually do a good one.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:56.160
And if you enjoy this video,

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:59.403
you should click right
here to see the next video.

